  "live_records": {
    "q-163014": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163014",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The mass density function $\\lambda ,$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ of a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $1\\le x\\le 2$ is given by $\\lambda (x)=4{x}^{3}-3,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?",
      "worked_solution": "Given a thin rod oriented along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by  \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=4{x}^{3}-3,\\,a=1,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}-3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{4}-3x]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(16-6)-(1-3) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $12\\,\\text{kg}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
