    "q-163010": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163010",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The mass density function $\\lambda ,$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ of a thin rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 2$ is given by $\\lambda (x)=x+2,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?",
      "worked_solution": "Given a thin rod oriented along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b,$ if $\\lambda (x)$ denotes the mass density function of the rod, then the total mass of the rod is given by  \n\n$$\nm={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=x+2,\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe calculate the mass of the rod as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x+2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{2}+2\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the rod is $6\\,\\text{kg}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
