    "q-163040": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163040",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "A thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)={x}^{2}+1,$ measured in $\\text{kg/m},$ is placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 2,$ where $x$ is measured in meters. Find the moment of the rod about the origin.",
      "worked_solution": "The moment about the origin $O$ of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda (x)$ for $a\\le x\\le b$ is given by \n\n$$\n{M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the moment of the rod about the origin as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}+x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the required moment is $6\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
