    "q-163029": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163029",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider a thin rod along the $x$-axis over the interval $0\\le x\\le 1$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)=6{x}^{2}+1.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass of the rod.",
      "worked_solution": "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=6{x}^{2}+1,\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}+x]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (6{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{3}+x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-163029-a",
