    "q-163020": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163020",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14120744/assets/ad5a7d1121f6ae5a471f78477a8857b75c6a5a2008e769b1b9115c2ac880d5b6.png)\n\nThe thin rod above has the mass density function $\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}.$ Given that the mass of the rod is $\\frac{9}{2}$ units, what is the $x$-coordinate of its center of mass?",
      "worked_solution": "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}},\\,a=1,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{8}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{8}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}]}_{1}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(32-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{93}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{93}{5})}{(\\frac{9}{2})}=\\frac{62}{15}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
